messages:
taylor_csm: FYI customer reported flaky EventSource in Safari for our /v1/stream endpoint. they see partial messages then connection dead. sending logs and headers.

emily_se: ah. do we have a repro URL + what CDN?

taylor_csm: Cloudflare in front, region=eu-west. reporter says it mainly hits Safari on iOS 16.5 and Safari desktop 15.4. other browsers (chrome/firefox) look fine.

aj_infra: can you paste the response headers and an excerpt of the raw bytes? Safari quirks often show up with compression or 304s.

emily_se: pasted snippets from the customer's capture:

```
GET /v1/stream?session=abc123 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.redwood.example
Accept: text/event-stream
Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate
Authorization: Bearer <redacted>
\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/event-stream; charset=utf-8
Content-Encoding: gzip
Transfer-Encoding: chunked
Cache-Control: public, max-age=60
ETag: "W/\"xyz\""
Connection: keep-alive
\n[data truncated - stream appears to be gzipped chunks]
```

lucas_frontend: 2 red flags: (1) Content-Encoding: gzip on SSE (2) Cache-Control public + ETag with CDN. Safari historically doesn't handle compressed streaming well — it buffers until gzip stream completes. also if CDN returns a 304 on revalidate it might close the connection.

aj_infra: agreed. Cloudflare + gzip + public caching is risky for SSE. CDN may try to cache/transform, edge might return 304, or send truncated chunk. Also some edges will re-compress which breaks streaming.

emily_se: customer reported that reloading the page sometimes resumes but most times they get partial event payloads (first few events ok), then after ~20s Safari stops emitting. chrome keeps streaming.

aj_infra: can we reproduce internally? lucas, can you spin a small server that intentionally gzips event-stream so we can test Safari?

lucas_frontend: on it. quick node repro:

```
// node minimal SSE with gzip
const http = require('http');
const zlib = require('zlib');
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
    'Cache-Control': 'public, max-age=60',
    'Content-Encoding': 'gzip',
  });
  const gz = zlib.createGzip();
  gz.pipe(res);
  let i=0;
  const t = setInterval(() => {
    i++;
    gz.write(`data: chunk ${i}\n\n`);
    if (i>50) { clearInterval(t); gz.end(); }
  }, 200);
}).listen(8088);
```

aj_infra: tested repro from macOS Safari 15.4 — it receives the first 2-3 events then stalls, console shows no more 'message' events. curl -N shows continuous output iff Content-Encoding removed.

ops_bot: deploy-bot: prod edge config for /v1/stream currently = Cloudflare cache: enabled, Edge compress: on, Edge cache: cache everything (query string), origin returns ETag.

emily_se: OK that aligns. root cause hypothesis: SSE response is being compressed and cached at the edge which results in buffering or truncated streams for Safari. Also ETag/304 interactions may cause the edge to serve a 304 without a body and Safari interprets that as end-of-stream.

aj_infra: remediation plan (short term): 1) instruct CDN to bypass caching and compression for SSE endpoints. set Cache-Control: no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate and/or add Cache-Control: private. set Surrogate-Control: no-store for CF workers/edge. 2) remove Content-Encoding for SSE (send identity) so browser can process chunks as they arrive. 3) add a health-checking retry header and adjust client reconnect logic to handle failed/closed streams gracefully.

lucas_frontend: client workaround we can ship quickly: use a tiny fetch-based polyfill that opens a long Polling fallback when EventSource stops emitting in Safari. But prefer server fix. polyfill example:

```
if (typeof EventSource === 'undefined' || navigator.userAgent.includes('Safari')) {
  // fallback: fetch + keep-alive chunk parsing
}
```

emily_se: ops, can you change edge rule for /v1/stream to: Cache-Control: no-store, Edge compress OFF, and ensure origin sends Content-Encoding: identity?
aj_infra: filing infra task and will roll change to staging first. patch will: set Worker rule to bypass cache for path prefix /v1/stream, set cf.edge.cacheTTL=0, and drop `accept-encoding`/prevent recompression. also add header "X-Stream: yes" to help tracing. ticket INFRA-1229.

ops_bot: deploy-bot: staged worker created, canary to 10% now.

emily_se: i18n note: some customers rely on auth in query string. long-term we should discourage auth tokens in query string for SSE because many CDNs key caching on querystring; tokens being in query leads to accidental caching. recommend using Authorization header with session cookie or proxy tunnel.

aj_infra: added to INFRA-1229: update docs + recommended best practices (Authorization header + short-lived cookies OR signed session with edge bypass).

lucas_frontend: also noticed gzip + chunking plus Content-Length missing is the usual problem: when gzip is used, browser waits for gzip footer to mark stream done. so effectively streaming is not streaming. big thumbs up to disabling compression for SSE.

emily_se: can we capture a failing trace for the customer? taylor, ask them to collect a HAR or tcpdump and include response start/stop times + edge id. we'll attach to INFRA-1229.

emily_se: temporary mitigation for this customer: proxy their SSE through a small service that strips `Accept-Encoding` and ensures `Cache-Control: no-store`. if they need a config snippet, i can paste an nginx example.

aj_infra: nginx snippet (for support reference):

```
location /v1/stream {
  proxy_pass http://origin;
  proxy_set_header Accept-Encoding ""; # disable gzip between client and origin
  proxy_set_header Connection "keep-alive";
  proxy_buffering off;
  add_header Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate" always;
}
```

ops_bot: deploy-bot: canary rollback if errors > 5/min. monitoring rule created: sse-client-errors and sse-connection-count per region.

taylor_csm: told customer we'll push edge config change and to try an immediate workaround (proxy snippet). they responded they'd test later this afternoon. i'll update ticket when they confirm. thanks team :heart: :+1:

emily_se: follow-ups: - INFRA-1229 to add edge rules + docs - DEVX to add SSE troubleshooting note in support KB (gzip + cache + safari) - Frontend to evaluate lightweight polyfill fallback if we see more flaky clients

lucas_frontend: will prototype a tiny fallback and post PR referencing INFRA-1229.

aj_infra: marking this thread as triaged. if the customer still fails after edge fixes, we'll collect HAR/pcap and escalate to CF support.

emily_se: thanks all. closing out from my side once INFRA ticket moves to ready-for-qa.

(Reaction notes: several :thumbsup: and :shipit: in thread)
